  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday through Monday: An upper level low pressure system \nwill continue to wobble around the Inland Northwest through \nMonday, keeping the region in a cool and showery weather \npattern. Perturbations that spin around the upper level low will\nprovide forcing for shower development. Surface heating each \nafternoon will increase lapse rates and initiate convective \nshower development as well. \n\nFor Saturday, the highest chances for showers (40-60% chance) \nwill be over the Cascades. Lower chances for showers (20-40%)  \nwill be found across the eastern third of WA, the northern WA \nmountains, and the ID Panhandle. There will be a 15-30% chance\nof accompanying thunderstorms mainly over mountain areas\nSaturday afternoon.\n\nBy Sunday, the highest precip chances shift further east across\nthe eastern third of WA and North ID. Precip chances ramp up to\nbetween 60 and 80% across Southeast WA and the ID Panhandle \nSunday night into Monday as PWATs increase to between 0.8-1.0 \ninches (around 150% of normal). \n\nIt's really tough to pin point how much precipitation will fall\nas there will be some convective elements in place with \nmodestly steep lapse rates through mid levels of the \natmosphere. There will be the potential for heavier showers that\nbring wetting rains (greater than a tenth of an inch), which\nwill largely be beneficial for the ongoing drought across the \nregion. Where rain may be a nuisance is if it gets heavy over a\nrecent burn scar, as that would bring a small chance for minor \nflooding impacts.\n\nIn addition to showers, Monday will feature breezy westerly\nwinds through the Cascade gaps and across the Columbia Basin\nwith sustained speeds of 10-15 mph and gusts to 25 mph.  \n\nAs for temperatures, afternoon highs will be in the low to mid\n70s Saturday through Monday. Morning low temperatures will \nrange from the 40s to low 50s. Breaks in the cloud cover each \nnight may yield patchy fog as rain will moisten up the boundary \nlayer.\n\nTuesday through Friday: Models start to diverge Tuesday onward, \nreducing confidence in the forecast for this timeframe, but the\ngeneral consensus is that the ridging over the Central U.S. \nwill build in toward the Inland Northwest, bringing a warming\nand drying trend for the middle to end of next week. /Fewkes",
